    "html": "<p>Two points R and S are in a uniform electric field of strength <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"E\"></span>, directed vertically downwards. The horizontal distance from R to S iThis question proved relatively challenging, with many students choosing incorrect options: C (using the<br/>total displacement instead of the displacement in the direction of the field) or D (misinterpreting the sign<br/>of the change the potential increases in the direction opposite to the field strength).<br/>s <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"Δ x\"></span>, and the vertical distance from R to S is <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"Δ y\"></span>.</p>\n<p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p>\n<p><br/>The electric potential at R is zero. What is the electric potential at S?</p>\n<p>[1]</p>\n<ol class=\"mcq-options\"><li data-label=\"A\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"E \\sqrt{Δ {x}^{2} + Δ {y}^{2}}\"></span></li><li data-label=\"B\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"E Δ y\"></span></li><li data-label=\"C\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"- E \\sqrt{Δ {x}^{2} + Δ {y}^{2}}\"></span></li><li data-label=\"D\"><span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"- E Δ y\"></span></li></ol>"
  },
